WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.170
Vsauce!

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:05.991
Kevin here -- with a math crime so sinister
the Michigan Supreme Court stated that no

00:00:05.991 --> 00:00:11.139
other game operated more extensively or demoralized
so many people.

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:18.090
City police forces repeatedly shut it down,
and the FBI waged all-out war against it.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:24.100
And it made criminals out of people like…
a Detroit mom named Miss Fannie.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.710
What they didn’t know is that they were
fighting against a nearly perfect game that

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.880
was about way more than just beating the odds.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:42.499
In Atlanta, it was called “Playing The Bug”
-- in Harlem and elsewhere, it was still called

00:00:42.499 --> 00:00:47.100
“Policy” after the original game that
drew numbers from 1 to 78.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:53.620
But the real name is just “Numbers” -- and
although it was associated with black communities,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.699
everyone played the Numbers.

00:00:55.699 --> 00:01:01.749
When Fannie Davis set up her Detroit neighborhood
Numbers game in 1958, there just wasn’t

00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:08.020
much economic opportunity for the black community
-- so, she started her own business.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.750
And she funded it after winning big on Numbers
herself.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.770
It worked like this.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:20.539
You’d place a bet on a 3-digit number from
000 to 999, and that could hit one of two

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:21.539
ways.

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:25.791
You could play it straight, where you hoped
to hit the exact number -- like, 607 -- and

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:30.280
you’d be paid 500 or 600 times your bet.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.350
Or you could bet a number “boxed,” meaning
you’d win if any combination of your numbers

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.350
hit.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:41.200
That’s 3!, so 3 x 2 x 1 = 6 possible combinations
[607, 670, 760, 706, 067, 076], and it would

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.140
pay about 80 to 1.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.140
Okay.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.140
So.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.829
How did they choose the winning number?

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:54.439
I’ve got a cage full of balls numbered 1
to 100 -- I’ll give it a crank and then

00:01:54.439 --> 00:01:55.689
I’ll draw three.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:57.549
I’m gonna play: 394.

00:01:57.549 --> 00:02:02.701
That’s my lucky number because .394 was
Tony Gwynn’s batting average in 1994 when

00:02:02.701 --> 00:02:08.250
he was on pace to hit over .400 for the first
time since Ted Williams hit .406 in 1941,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.709
but a labor strike shortened the baseball
season and RUINED EVERYTHING.

00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:12.769
Okay.

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:17.030
Here we go… 32.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.950
I already lost.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:30.780
Oh well let’s keep going.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.740
Oh.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:38.640
My cage of balls broke.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:39.850
Oh.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.819
I am a fool.

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:44.800
Oh there’s so many on the floor.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.780
Alright let’s finish this off.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.129
Stay closed.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:52.799
86.

00:02:52.799 --> 00:02:56.150
Last number.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.819
91.

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:04.599
The winning number was 261, my number is 394.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:05.939
Sorry, Tony...

00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:08.010
But… this method is silly.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.780
No one would trust a guy pulling balls out
of a cage in his house.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.310
To get the winning number in a more fair manner…

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:20.120
I could do something like… take 3 YouTube
videos, reload them each day on a livestream

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.409
and use the ones digit from the view counts
to pick the winning number.

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:28.120
And Fannie’s game worked kinda similar to
that.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.460
She used the “mutuel” from a local racetrack
-- the betting totals for win, place, and

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.740
show on a series of horse races.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.939
She’d take the ones digit from the 4th,
5th, and 6th race totals, and there’s your

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:41.050
3 numbers.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:48.390
It was technically possible to influence those
digits, but incredibly difficult -- and expensive.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.470
Other games used independent numbers like
digits from the published daily balance of

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:57.019
the US Treasury -- things you could open up
a newspaper and see.

00:03:57.019 --> 00:04:02.799
Look, here’s the real beauty of Numbers
and why it worked so well for Fannie and her

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:10.500
community: You win just often enough to enjoy
playing, and to validate every possible betting

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:11.500
system.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:16.940
If you play 6 days a week you’ll probably
win boxed 2 or 3 times a year, and you’ll

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.410
hit straight for 500 times your bet every
few years.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.500
Did a number come to you in a dream?

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.160
Do you think you have a scientific lottery-beating
system?

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.790
They all end up winning.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:35.860
And yeah, the house edge was steep -- getting
paid 500 to 1 on something you know has a

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.880
1 in 1,000 chance of happening means someone’s
making money somewhere.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.480
They’re also taking bets, keeping meticulous
records, collecting money from losses and

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.530
distributing money from wins -- all as they’re
calculating their own mathematical risk of

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:56.680
ruin in real-time to make sure they can survive
financially if someone hits big.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:03.830
But for Fannie’s neighbors, they could see
$1 become $500 overnight.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:10.550
The FBI saw an opportunity to rip it away
from them and give it to… someone else.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.480
We’ll get to that in a second.

