00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.440
Okay.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:18.830
Law enforcement called the Numbers game racketeering,
and in 1970 the FBI led what J Edgar Hoover

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:26.470
called the largest gambling raid in history,
shutting down 58 Numbers operators.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:33.020
Then governments realized that if the house
always wins, they could just… become the

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.470
house.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:40.060
States made Numbers illegal, did their best
to wipe it out, and then gave themselves a

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.300
legal Numbers monopoly.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.300
Wow!

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.130
What a neat trick!

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:51.350
Lotteries have actually had a really complex
history in the United States -- from the colonial

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:56.840
period to early statehood, hundreds of lotteries
helped raise money for schools and public

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:57.840
works.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.660
By the 1830’s, lotteries were denounced
by social reformers as morally bankrupt, predatory

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:12.010
games that generated “tainted revenue”
-- and in 1895 lottery games were banned nationwide.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:18.490
From legal lotteries to policy houses, there
was enough corruption and game-fixing to consider

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.360
what was best for the public interest.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:28.810
Now 44 states have legalized lotteries, and
the Powerball is played in all of them -- to

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.950
help raise money for schools and public works.

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:35.020
And we’ve come full circle.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:41.910
But the reality is to run a game like Numbers,
what you really need is the trust of your

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:42.910
community.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:48.420
Fannie Davis and other boutique Numbers operators
engaged in a sort of prescient scholarship

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:54.710
decades before neuroscience and psychology
research confirmed what they harnessed intuitively

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:58.990
-- the community element of Numbers was everything.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:04.340
They seized the power of Dunbar’s Number,
building on the social cohesion in well-connected

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:10.590
neighborhoods so everyone could play a game
together — and that’s completely lost

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:16.040
when 100,000 strangers play a lottery anonymously
at a gas station.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:22.020
They knew that throwing in just one additional
number meant only hitting straight once every

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.740
30 years -- that’s probably once in a lifetime.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:32.260
And that’s not often enough for most people
to wanna keep playing, even if it increases

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.250
the prizes dramatically.

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:40.709
They intuited the communal strength of Rene
Girard’s mimetic desire, and how much people

00:08:40.709 --> 00:08:45.329
love playing games together -- and need to.

00:08:45.329 --> 00:08:51.490
And they actually knew how to make a small
economy work: Numbers created tens of thousands

00:08:51.490 --> 00:09:00.060
of jobs within communities trying to survive
in the face of discrimination, segregation

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:01.920
and rampant racism.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:09.379
A 1903 Detroit Free Press article said Numbers
was “largely the gambling field of the ignorant,

00:09:09.379 --> 00:09:16.350
superstitious, and poor… the main thing
is to have the evil eternally wiped out.”

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:22.720
Lottery ban legislation was a patronizing
attempt to protect communities from themselves.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.630
Now we’ve got Japan’s Takarakuji, South
Korea’s Lotto 6/45, the US Mega Millions

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:34.560
and Russia’s Gosloto -- in addition to US
state games that operate exactly like Numbers

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.720
did decades ago.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.200
Pennsylvania even named their 3-digit lottery
“The Daily Number.”

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:48.529
But those games can’t do what people like
Fannie Davis did in Detroit.

00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:54.470
There’s no talk at the diner about who won
that week or who hasn’t hit in ages.

00:09:54.470 --> 00:10:00.399
There’s no knock on the door to deliver
your lucky payout the day after your numbers

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:01.399
match.

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:07.990
There’s no playing 357 because you live
at 357 Park Drive and everyone knowing when

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:11.240
that number hit – YOU were the winner.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.040
That’s all gone.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:18.509
There’s enough intrigue about Fannie Davis’s
life and her Numbers game to fill a great

00:10:18.509 --> 00:10:21.910
docuseries -- and there were thousands like
her.

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:27.209
Read Bridgett Davis’s book about her mom
-- that’s her story to tell, not mine.

00:10:27.209 --> 00:10:32.250
Check out Felicia George’s dissertation
about Numbers in Detroit, or Louise Meriwether’s

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:37.550
novel “Daddy was a Number Runner” -- you’ve
heard a million stories about the good guys

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.760
and bad guys during Prohibition.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:45.450
But Numbers stories like Miss Fannie’s have
been almost entirely ignored.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.079
But they shouldn’t be.

00:10:48.079 --> 00:10:54.509
Because alongside the rackets were people
who understood the social fabric of their

00:10:54.509 --> 00:10:55.600
communities.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:59.800
Making your own way in the world and making
everyone else a little bit happier?

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:03.939
That’s not the American Dream, that’s
everyone’s dream.

00:11:03.939 --> 00:11:10.139
But the highly-regulated mass-production of
fun strips the complex social function from

00:11:10.139 --> 00:11:14.980
a game that’s the perfect blend of math
and people.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:20.060
It’s reduced to lonely, anonymous gambling.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:25.970
And when that happens, the only thing left
is just… numbers.

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:29.639
And as always, thanks for watching.

